  "description": "A 60-second lesson on Higher-Order Theories.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "You see a red apple. The distinction matters.",
      "visual": "🍎"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Higher-order theories say a mental state becomes conscious only when the brain forms a meta-representation of that state.",
      "visual": "🔄"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "First-order perception is processing the apple. Only the second step creates conscious experience.",
      "visual": "📝"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "This means animals or brain regions lack higher-order representation may process information without ever experiencing it.",
      "visual": "🤖"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Damage to it can impair self-awareness while leaving perception intact.",
      "visual": "🏥"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Consciousness may not be about seeing the world.",
      "visual": "👁️"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What do higher-order theories require for conscious experience?",
    "options": [
      "A representation of the mental state itself",
      "Direct sensory input from the environment",
      "Activation of the brainstem"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
